`Board.reveal(cell)` starts a flood-fill whenever the clicked cell has zero adjacent mines — otherwise it reveals just that one cell.

```java
void reveal(Cell cell) {
    if (cell.isRevealed() || cell.isFlagged()) return;
    cell.setRevealed(true);
    game.decrementRemainingSafeCells();

    if (cell.isMine()) {
        game.onMineRevealed(); // loss
        return;
    }
    if (cell.getAdjacentMineCount() == 0) {
        for (Cell neighbor : getNeighbors(cell)) {
            reveal(neighbor); // flood-fill continues through zero-count cells
        }
    }
}
```

The recursion only continues through cells with a zero count — a cell bordering a mine (count > 0) is revealed but doesn't propagate further, which is exactly why Minesweeper boards show numbers at the edge of a revealed region.

Each cell's adjacent-mine count is computed **once**, right after mine placement, and cached — never recalculated by scanning the board on each reveal.

```java
void computeAdjacentCounts() {
    for (Cell cell : allCells) {
        if (cell.isMine()) continue;
        int count = 0;
        for (Cell neighbor : getNeighbors(cell)) {
            if (neighbor.isMine()) count++;
        }
        cell.setAdjacentMineCount(count);
    }
}
```

This runs exactly once, right after the first-click mine placement (§6) — an O(cells × 8) pass, since each cell has at most 8 neighbors. `reveal()` and the flood-fill afterward only ever *read* `getAdjacentMineCount()`, never recompute it.
